{"id":"1628972","metadata":{"publication_info":[{"year":2022,"page_end":"197","page_start":"97","journal_issue":"1","journal_title":"J.Diff.Geom.","journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1214449"},"journal_volume":"120"}],"citation_count":22,"citation_count_without_self_citations":18,"dois":[{"value":"10.4310/jdg/1641413698","source":"Lehigh University"}],"titles":[{"title":"Instability of spherical naked singularities of a scalar field under gravitational perturbations","source":"Lehigh University"},{"title":"Instability of spherical naked singularities of a scalar field under gravitational perturbations","source":"arXiv"},{"title":"Instability of spherical naked singularities of a scalar field under isotropic gravitational perturbations","source":"arXiv"}],"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","authors":[{"uuid":"9f9e0ae4-7e7c-4884-9a40-c3366d17a0b5","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1945438"},"full_name":"Li, Junbin","signature_block":"Lj"},{"uuid":"9e201eb0-bbf7-40af-b250-0eca45d0e5b7","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1935750"},"full_name":"Liu, Jue","signature_block":"Lj"}],"curated":false,"figures":[{"key":"f61abd8195a9a18dcb9180475b748b27","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/f61abd8195a9a18dcb9180475b748b27","source":"arxiv","caption":"Basic setup","filename":"setup.png"},{"key":"5b9721f98a2db46ae64a2e32701250d5","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/5b9721f98a2db46ae64a2e32701250d5","source":"arxiv","caption":"The conclusions of Theorem \\ref{instabilityrough} and \\ref{instabilityrough2}","filename":"instability.png"}],"license":[{"url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","imposing":"arXiv"}],"texkeys":["Li:2017rgs"],"citeable":true,"imprints":[{"date":"2022-01-01"}],"refereed":true,"abstracts":[{"value":"In this paper, we initiate the study of the instability of naked singularities beyond spherical symmetry. In a series of papers, Christodoulou proved that naked singularities are not stable in the context of the spherically symmetric Einstein equations coupled with a massless scalar field. We study in this paper the next simplest case: a characteristic initial value problem of this coupled system with the initial data given on two intersecting null cones, the incoming one of which is assumed to be spherically symmetric and singular at its vertex, and the outgoing one of which has no symmetries. It is shown that, arbitrarily fixing the initial scalar field, the set of the initial conformal metrics on the outgoing null cone such that the maximal future development does not have any sequences of closed trapped surfaces approaching the singularity, is of first category in the whole space in which the shear tensors are continuous. Such a set can then be viewed as exceptional, although the exceptionality is weaker than the at least $1$ co‑dimensionality in spherical symmetry. Almost equivalently, it is also proved that, arbitrarily fixing an incoming null cone $\\underline{C}_{\\,\\varepsilon}$ to the future of the initial incoming null cone, the set of the initial conformal metrics such that the maximal future development has at least one closed trapped surface before $\\underline{C}_{\\,\\varepsilon}$, contains an open and dense subset of the whole space. Since the initial scalar field can be chosen such that the singularity is naked if the initial shear is set to be zero, we may say that the spherical naked singularities of a self-gravitating scalar field are not stable under gravitational perturbations. This in particular gives new families of non-spherically symmetric gravitational perturbations different from the original spherically symmetric scalar perturbations given by Christodoulou.","source":"Lehigh University"},{"value":"In this paper, we initiate the study of the instability of naked singularities without symmetries. In a series of papers, Christodoulou proved that naked singularities are not stable in the context of the spherically symmetric Einstein equations coupled with a massless scalar field. We study in this paper the next simplest case: a characteristic initial value problem of this coupled system with the initial data given on two intersecting null cones, the incoming one of which is assumed to be spherically symmetric and singular at its vertex, and the outgoing one of which has no symmetries. It is shown that, arbitrarily fixing the initial scalar field, the set of the initial conformal metrics on the outgoing null cone such that the maximal future development does not have any sequences of closed trapped surfaces approaching the singularity, is of first category in the whole space in which the shear tensors are continuous. Such a set can then be viewed as exceptional, although the exceptionality is weaker than the at least $1$ co-dimensionality in spherical symmetry. Almost equivalently, it is also proved that, arbitrarily fixing an incoming null cone $\\underline{C}_\\varepsilon$ to the future of the initial incoming null cone, the set of the initial conformal metrics such that the maximal future development has at least one closed trapped surface before $\\underline{C}_\\varepsilon$, contains an open and dense subset of the whole space. Since the initial scalar field can be chosen such that the singularity is naked if the initial shear is set to be zero, we may say that the spherical naked singularities of a self-gravitating scalar field are not stable under gravitational perturbations. This in particular gives new families of non-spherically symmetric gravitational perturbations different from the original spherically symmetric scalar perturbations given by Christodoulou.","source":"arXiv"}],"references":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1123487"},"reference":{"misc":["Formation of trapped surfaces from past null infinity, preprint"],"label":"1","authors":[{"full_name":"An, X."}],"arxiv_eprint":"1207.5271","publication_info":{"year":2012}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1318472"},"reference":{"misc":["Trapped surfaces in vacuum arising dynamically from mild incoming radiation"],"label":"2","authors":[{"full_name":"An, X."},{"full_name":"Luk, J."}],"publication_info":{"year":2017,"page_end":"120","page_start":"1","journal_title":"Adv.Theor.Math.Phys.","journal_volume":"21"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/249214"},"reference":{"misc":["of Gravitational Collapse"],"label":"3","authors":[{"full_name":"Christodoulou, D."},{"full_name":"Theory, A. Mathematical"}],"publication_info":{"year":1987,"page_end":"647","page_start":"613","journal_title":"Commun.Math.Phys.","journal_volume":"109"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1235882"},"reference":{"misc":["The formation of black holes and singularities in spherically symmetric gravitational collapse"],"label":"4","authors":[{"full_name":"Christodoulou, D."}],"publication_info":{"year":1991,"page_end":"373","page_start":"339","journal_title":"Commun.Pure Appl.Math.","journal_volume":"44"}},"curated_relation":false},{"reference":{"misc":["Bounded variation solutions of the spherically symmetric einstein-scalar field equations"],"label":"5","authors":[{"full_name":"Christodoulou, D."}],"publication_info":{"year":1993,"page_end":"1220","page_start":"1131","journal_title":"Commun.Pure Appl.Math.","journal_volume":"46"}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/391017"},"reference":{"misc":["Examples of Naked Singularity Formation in the Gravitational Collapse of a Scalar Field"],"label":"6","authors":[{"full_name":"Christodoulou, D."}],"publication_info":{"year":1994,"page_end":"653","page_start":"607","journal_title":"Annals Math.","journal_volume":"140"}},"curated_relation":false},{"reference":{"misc":["On the global initial value problem and the issue of singularities"],"label":"7","authors":[{"full_name":"Christodoulou, D."}],"publication_info":{"year":1999,"artid":"A23","page_start":"A23","journal_title":"Class.Quant.Grav.","journal_volume":"16"}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2946377"},"reference":{"misc":["The instability of naked singularities in the gravitational collapse of a scalar field"],"label":"8","authors":[{"full_name":"Christodoulou, D."}],"publication_info":{"year":1999,"page_end":"217","page_start":"183","journal_title":"Annals Math.","journal_volume":"149"}}},{"reference":{"misc":["The Formation of Black Holes in General Relativity, Monographs in Mathematics, European Mathematical Soc"],"label":"9","authors":[{"full_name":"Christodoulou, D."}],"publication_info":{"year":2009}}},{"reference":{"misc":["The Global Nonlinear Stability of Minkowski Space"],"label":"10","authors":[{"full_name":"Christodoulou, D."},{"full_name":"Klainerman, S."}],"publication_info":{"artid":"1993","page_start":"1993","journal_title":"Princeton Math.Ser.","journal_volume":"41"}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/645829"},"reference":{"misc":["Spherically symmetric spacetimes with a trapped surface"],"label":"11","authors":[{"full_name":"Dafermos, M."}],"publication_info":{"year":2005,"artid":"2221","page_start":"2221","journal_title":"Class.Quant.Grav.","journal_volume":"22"}},"curated_relation":false},{"reference":{"misc":["Ideal point in space-time, Proc. Roy. Soc. Lond. Ser. A 327 , 545-567"],"label":"12","authors":[{"full_name":"Geroch, R.P."},{"full_name":"Kronheimer, E.H."},{"full_name":"Penrose, R."}],"publication_info":{"year":1972}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1221031"},"reference":{"misc":["A fully anisotropic mechanism for formation of trapped surfaces in vacuum"],"label":"13","authors":[{"full_name":"Klainerman, S."},{"full_name":"Luk, J."},{"full_name":"Rodnianski, I."}],"publication_info":{"year":2014,"page_end":"26","page_start":"1","journal_title":"Invent.Math.","journal_volume":"198"}},"curated_relation":false},{"reference":{"misc":["On the Formation of Trapped Surfaces"],"label":"14","authors":[{"full_name":"Klainerman, S."},{"full_name":"Rodnianski, I."}],"publication_info":{"year":2012,"page_end":"333","page_start":"211","journal_title":"Acta Math.","journal_volume":"208"}}},{"reference":{"misc":["The bounded L2 curvature conjecture"],"label":"15","authors":[{"full_name":"Klainerman, S."},{"full_name":"Rodnianski, I."},{"full_name":"Szeftel, J."}],"publication_info":{"year":2015,"page_end":"216","page_start":"91","journal_title":"Invent.Math.","journal_volume":"202"}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1629145"},"reference":{"misc":["A robust proof of the instability of naked singularities of a scalar field in spherical symmetry, preprint"],"label":"16","authors":[{"full_name":"Li, J."},{"full_name":"Liu, J."}],"arxiv_eprint":"1710.02922"},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1298790"},"reference":{"misc":["On the local extension of the future null infinity to appear in Journal of Differential Geometry"],"label":"17","authors":[{"full_name":"Li, J."},{"full_name":"Zhu, X.P."}],"arxiv_eprint":"1406.0048"},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1345809"},"reference":{"misc":["Local existence in retarded time under a weak decay on complete null cones"],"label":"18","authors":[{"full_name":"Li, J."},{"full_name":"Zhu, X.P."}],"publication_info":{"year":2016,"page_end":"106","page_start":"85","journal_title":"Sci.China Math.","journal_volume":"59"}},"curated_relation":false},{"reference":{"misc":["Construction of Cauchy data of vacuum Einstein field equations evolving to black holes, Annals of Mathematics, Volume 181 , Issue 2, 699-768"],"label":"19","authors":[{"full_name":"Li, J."},{"full_name":"Yu, P."}],"publication_info":{"year":2015}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1265214"},"reference":{"misc":["Weak null singularities in general relativity, preprint"],"label":"20","authors":[{"full_name":"Luk, J."}],"arxiv_eprint":"1311.4970","publication_info":{"year":2013}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1184616"},"reference":{"misc":["Local propagation of impulsive gravitational waves"],"label":"21","authors":[{"full_name":"Luk, J."},{"full_name":"Rodnianski, I."}],"publication_info":{"year":2015,"page_end":"624","page_start":"511","journal_title":"Commun.Pure Appl.Math.","journal_volume":"68"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1209547"},"reference":{"misc":["Nonlinear interaction of impulsive gravitational waves for the vacuum Einstein equations preprint"],"label":"22","authors":[{"full_name":"Luk, J."},{"full_name":"Rodnianski, I."}],"arxiv_eprint":"1301.1072","publication_info":{"year":2013}},"curated_relation":false},{"reference":{"misc":["Reduction of the characteristic initial value problem to the Cauchy problem and its applications to the Einstein equations"],"label":"23","authors":[{"full_name":"Rendall, A."}],"publication_info":{"year":1990,"page_end":"239","page_start":"221","journal_title":"Proc.Roy.Soc.Lond.A","journal_volume":"427"}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/901978"},"reference":{"misc":["Dynamical Formation of black holes due to the condensation of matter field Department of Mathematics, Sun Yat-sen University, Guangzhou, China E-mail address: lijunbin@mail.sysu.edu.cn Department of Mathematics, Sun Yat-sen University, Guangzhou, China E-mail address: liuj337@mail2.sysu.edu.cn"],"label":"24","authors":[{"full_name":"Yu, P."}],"arxiv_eprint":"1105.5898"},"curated_relation":false}],"public_notes":[{"value":"88 pages, 2 figures. In this new version, the main result is replaced by a statement on all (not only isotropic) perturbations with exceptionality being of first category, weaker than being of co-dimension at least 1. The title changes according to this","source":"arXiv"}],"arxiv_eprints":[{"value":"1710.02422","categories":["gr-qc","math.AP","math.DG"]}],"document_type":["article"],"preprint_date":"2017-10-06","control_number":1628972,"legacy_version":"20200929220430.0","number_of_pages":86,"inspire_categories":[{"term":"Gravitation and Cosmology"},{"term":"Math and Math Physics"}],"legacy_creation_date":"2017-10-09","external_system_identifiers":[{"value":"4362287","schema":"MSNET"}]},"uuid":"55ed6d0b-9489-49f5-a1df-e0564c1375c2","links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1628972?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1628972?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1628972?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1628972?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1628972?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1628972?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A1628972"},"updated":"2025-08-21T08:18:54.956305+00:00","revision_id":104,"created":"2017-10-09T00:00:00+00:00"}